Agreed. That is what the series below does.

> I don't understand why they use these mechanisms. Is keeping the list in
> git not sufficient?

For us it is sufficient. The notmuch community needs these tools
because they don't have what we have.

Their list is not in git. Each developer gets the list by SMTP into
their own mailbox, mixed in with their other mail. File names, paths,
and even which messages are present differ from person to person and
from machine to machine. The only thing that is the same everywhere is
the Message-ID. notmuch keeps tags only in its Xapian database, which
is opaque, per machine, and can't be merged. So any state that should
outlive a database or travel to another person or machine has to go
somewhere else, keyed by Message-ID:

- nmbug shares tags among developers whose copies of the list are
  separate deliveries.
- muchsync syncs one person's mail and tags between their machines.
- notmuch dump/restore backs up the one copy of the tags, which is the
  database itself.
- Flag sync shares state with IMAP clients, which read seen and
  replied from the file names.

Our list is one git tree that everyone checks out byte for byte, at
the same paths. So state can live in the tree itself, as where a file
sits or who replied to it, and every tag is derived from that. Nothing
lives only in the database. That is why we don't need any of these
tools.

The limit is per-person state. Anything in the tree is shared, so
"archived" means archived for everyone. Private state like unread or
flagged has no place in the shared branch. If we ever want that,
notmuch-git with a per-user prefix is the tool for it.

