Done, on a scratch copy of the list, the database, and the repo. First,
a correction: I said notmuch-git was "already installed here". It is
not. The FreeBSD package ships only the man page, not the script. I
ran notmuch-git.py from the 0.40 release tarball under python3.12. It
needs only the standard library; the notmuch2 bindings are optional.

What works: init, commit, push, clone into a second repo, and checkout
after a rebuilt database. Each tag is one empty file, e.g.
tags/4b/1c/m2ld85wpae.fsf@beastie.p7x.dev/archived. With the prefix set
to lab::, only lab:: tags sync, and the derived tags stay out.

Rough edges:

- status and commit fail with "no upstream configured" until the branch
  has an upstream. The script checks for the error text with a capital
  N, which current git no longer prints.
- The default git.safe_fraction is 0.1. On a list of ten messages,
  archiving one already trips it, so every commit needs --force or a
  config change.
- After rebuilding the database, checkout silently restores nothing
  until you delete the cache under <repo>/notmuch/.
- Another correction: a missing message shows as D, not U as I said
  before. U means a tag only upstream has. When the message comes back,
  its tag is gone from the database, so a commit in between deletes the
  tag from git too.

As a ref in the jj repo: not directly. notmuch-git uses the git dir's
own index file and update-ref HEAD. Pointed at our colocated .git, it
would overwrite jj's index and move jj's HEAD. A linked worktree does
work:

    git worktree add --no-checkout <path> lab-tags
    NOTMUCH_GIT_DIR=.git/worktrees/<name> notmuch-git commit --force

The worktree has its own HEAD and index, and shares refs and objects.
Commits land on refs/heads/lab-tags. The main index and HEAD stay
untouched, jj imports lab-tags as a bookmark, and jj git push carries
it to a remote. I did not test merging tag edits from two people
through jj.

> Also determine whether it seems like a shared archive dir is an
> effective option for right now, or if moving to notmuch-git(1) right
> away would be more effective. Provide a patch series for whichever you
> pick.

The shared archive dir. It keeps every tag derived from the mail branch
alone. It needs no new tool, no second ref, and no separate commit step.
Archiving is a commit like any other mail, and the moderator already
merges those. notmuch-git works, but today it would mean packaging the
script and working around the four rough edges above. None of that buys
anything until we want per-person state. Moving files also avoids the
symlink problem: nothing can dangle, so notmuch new can't fail for
everyone.

The series is "[PATCH 0/3] Archive messages that need no answer" [1].
Patch 1 tags files under maildir/archive/ as archived and drops inbox.
Patch 2 documents archiving in zib/patch-review-workflow.org. It also
relaxes the two rules that would have rejected an archive commit: the
moderator's "only add maildir messages" check, and the note that a
message file is never renamed. Patch 3 lets process-email archive a
message that asks nothing, instead of replying.

In tests, archiving clears inbox and the tag survives a rebuilt
database. Unarchiving restores inbox, and a replied message keeps
replied. jj records the move as one rename, and two people archiving
the same message merge without conflict.

One unrelated thing I hit this pass. Step 1 of process-email runs "jj
rebase -b claude -d mail@upstream", then "jj new claude". When all of
my outbox has already been merged into mail, there is nothing to
rebase, so the bookmark stays behind mail@upstream. The pass then works
on the old list and misses new mail. Here it hid your message until I
moved onto mail@upstream by hand. I'll send a fix as its own patch
unless you'd rather handle it differently.

